\caption(Gaussian denoising and patch completion have different inputs. The masked composite copies observations at visible locations and uses model predictions only at hidden locations.)
\alttext(A denoising branch maps a corrupted observation through a frozen dense network. A separate masked branch encodes visible patches, decodes predictions and combines hidden predictions with exact visible observations.)

> Four patches make the copy rule checkable

Let hidden mask h=[0,1,0,1], observed patch values o=[2,9,4,9] and predictions p=[6,7,8,9].
The composite is [2,7,4,9]. Hidden placeholders never become observed evidence. With target
x=[2,1,4,3], hidden-only MSE is ((7−1)²+(9−3)²)/2=36; full-composite MSE is 18 because half
the coordinates are hidden. The actual application hides twelve of sixteen equal-size patches,
so its full-composite MSE is 0.75 times its hidden-only MSE.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/restoration_copy_rule.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n           = dm::nn;
  const auto t          = n::DType::Float64;
  const auto observed   = n::tensor({2., 9., 4., 9.}, {1, 4, 1}, t),
             prediction = n::tensor({6., 7., 8., 9.}, {1, 4, 1}, t),
             hidden     = n::tensor({0., 1., 0., 1.}, {1, 4}, t),
             target     = n::tensor({2., 1., 4., 3.}, {1, 4, 1}, t);
  const auto visible    = n::models::VisiblePatchValues(observed, hidden),
             composite  = n::models::CompositePatchValues(visible, prediction, hidden);
  const double hm       = n::models::MaeLoss(composite, target, hidden).item(),
               full     = n::mean(n::square(composite - target)).item();
  if (hm != 36 || full != 18)
    return 1;
  std::print(
      "| patch | hidden | observed placeholder | prediction | composite "
      "|\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n");
  dm::graphics::Diagram          d(40, 1100);
  dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;
  style.width     = 4.2;
  style.height    = 2.4;
  style.font_size = 20;
  for (int k = 0; k < 4; ++k) {
    auto st          = style;
    st.fill          = k % 2 ? dm::RGB{255, 237, 213} : dm::RGB{219, 234, 254};
    const auto value = composite.at({0, k, 0});
    d.node(
        "p" + std::to_string(k),
        {k * 5.6, 0},
        std::format("Patch{}: {}\n{}", k, value, k % 2 ? "Predicted hidden" : "Copied visible"),
        st);
    std::print(
        "| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",
        k,
        hidden.at({0, k}),
        observed.at({0, k, 0}),
        prediction.at({0, k, 0}),
        value);
  }
  d.text(
       {8.4, -4},
       "Hidden MSE 36; full MSE 18 = one-half hidden MSE.\nThe original application uses hidden "
       "fraction 12/16 = 0.75.",
       20)
      .save_svg("build/restoration_copy_rule.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| patch | hidden | observed placeholder | prediction | composite |
|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| 0 | 0 | 2 | 6 | 2 |
| 1 | 1 | 9 | 7 | 7 |
| 2 | 0 | 4 | 8 | 4 |
| 3 | 1 | 9 | 9 | 9 |
\image(figures/restoration_copy_rule.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The blue patches copy observed values exactly; orange patches use predictions. Arithmetic blending is unnecessary, so signed zeros in selected values are preserved too.)
\alttext(Four coloured patch cells show copied visible values 2 and 4, with predicted hidden values 7 and 9. The hidden-only and full-error denominators are stated below.)

The native generic functions accept finite signed Float32/64[B,P,D] patches and a detached
same-dtype binary mask[B,P], with one meaning hidden. They support strides, all-visible and
all-hidden masks, preserve selected values and retain selected continuous derivatives.
Every value must be finite, including unselected branches. For image inference, MaePatches
and MaeImage provide the fixed 7×7 tile mapping. A hidden mask is a location indicator, not
a learned soft blending coefficient.

> Reproduce the three prerequisite checkpoints locally

The four complete programs below prepare genuine MNIST through the native configurable cache,
then repeat the original three-epoch fits. They reproduce all 115,633 saved parameter values
and the three checkpoint archives byte-for-byte. This preparation has 288 visible Adam updates:
96 for each model. The restoration application that follows adds **zero** updates. No frozen
weights are downloaded, embedded, manually placed or trained inside a preparation helper.

All pools contain 1000 fit, 200 validation and 200 held-out test images, balanced by digit.
Labels and source IDs describe provenance and masks; labels never enter autoencoder fitting.
The two dense networks share seed 1657, 784→32 ReLU→784 sigmoid, 50,992 scalars and matched
batch ordering. Each makes 96 Adam(.003) steps; the denoiser sees fresh sigma 0.35 corruption,
while the ordinary control sees clean inputs. Both select on the same fixed noisy validation
images and clean targets. The masked model has 13,649 scalars/35 leaves, two-head widths 32/16,
four visible 7×7 patches and one block per side. Seed 1877, order 1889 and source-keyed masks
reproduce its 96 steps. Validation hidden-pixel MSE selects its checkpoint.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n = dm::nn;
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::filesystem::path cache = dm::datasets::data_home();
  dm::datasets::prepare_example_data("mnist-assets", cache.string());
  const auto pools           = n::models::LoadSaliency(cache / "saliency");
  const auto validation_bank = n::models::GaussianNoise(pools[1].images.shape(), 1, 1709);
  const auto test_bank       = n::models::GaussianNoise(pools[2].images.shape(), 1, 1711);
  const auto second_bank     = n::models::GaussianNoise(pools[2].images.shape(), 1, 1717);
  const auto validation = n::models::CorruptForDenoising(pools[1].images, validation_bank, .35f);
  const auto test       = n::models::CorruptForDenoising(pools[2].images, test_bank, .35f);
  n::save_npz(
      "build/denoising_data.npz",
      {{"fit_images", pools[0].images},
       {"validation_images", pools[1].images},
       {"test_images", pools[2].images},
       {"fit_source_ids", pools[0].source},
       {"validation_source_ids", pools[1].source},
       {"test_source_ids", pools[2].source},
       {"test_labels", pools[2].labels},
       {"validation_standard_noise", validation_bank},
       {"test_standard_noise", test_bank},
       {"second_standard_noise", second_bank},
       {"validation_corrupted", validation},
       {"test_corrupted", test},
       {"test_low", n::models::CorruptForDenoising(pools[2].images, test_bank, .15f)},
       {"test_strong", n::models::CorruptForDenoising(pools[2].images, test_bank, .6f)},
       {"test_second", n::models::CorruptForDenoising(pools[2].images, second_bank, .35f)}});
  std::filesystem::copy_file(
      cache / "saliency" / "manifest.json",
      "build/denoising_data_manifest.json",
      std::filesystem::copy_options::overwrite_existing);
  std::print("| pool | images | pixels per image | examples per digit |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n");
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i)
    std::print(
        "| {} | {} | 784 | {} |\n",
        i == 0   ? "fit"
        : i == 1 ? "validation"
                 : "test",
        pools[i].images.size(0),
        pools[i].images.size(0) / 10);
}
